From c070b7b141bd7adb6dc7736b2a4a4217b8b7b27d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 09:27:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Decide RegexOptions.Compiled per pattern by racing both forms
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 5's item 2 declined to ship a `Compiled` policy because one of
seven real Octane patterns — an ordinary trim — measured 4.3x SLOWER
compiled, which kills "compile after N uses". It closed by naming the
design most likely to survive a wider corpus: measure both forms once on
the real subject and keep the winner, because that needs no predicate.

This is that design. A JSRegExp counts its own matches; at the thousandth
the engine builds the compiled form, times both arms ABBA-interleaved on
the subject in hand under a 4 ms budget, and adopts the compiled one only
if it wins by 1.15x. Verdicts are cached by (pattern, options) so sibling
instances of a literal inherit rather than race again. It never
re-decides and never touches a pattern routed to Broiler.Regex.

The arm the race picks is unobservable: RegexOptions.Compiled changes
code generation and nothing else, so the two matchers are built from one
pattern and one option set. RegexTieringTests asserts that directly — 15
cases run on BOTH settings and required to agree, covering captures and
their order, named groups, lastIndex across a global exec loop, sticky in
both directions, a backreference, replace with a function, split, the
Annex B statics, a gap-feature pattern that must never race, and
RegExp.prototype.compile replacing a matcher that has already gone hot.
All 15 pass on the unmodified engine too.

Off by default (BROILER_JS_REGEX_TIERING=1), because measuring it found
no speed-up to justify a retained DynamicMethod per hot pattern: 1.010x
on 3 of 6 interleaved pairs of Octane's RegExp suite, which is the only
one of fifteen it reaches — eleven build no regex at all.

Three probes come with it, and the third is the one that matters:

  --regex-tiering        the eleven phase-5 patterns through the ENGINE
                         on both settings, with each race's verdict
  --regex-call-envelope  the same work through re.test / re.exec /
                         String.prototype.search and through Regex.IsMatch
                         and Regex.Match directly, at one iteration count
  --specializing-tier    regexPatternsBuilt / regexRaces / regexRacesPromoted
    ... counters         / regexVerdictsReused, plus per-race rows

The envelope says why nothing aimed at matching can move this suite: the
matcher is 4.6-6.5% of what re.test costs and 8.7-9.4% of what re.exec
allocates. A regex call pays a fixed ~2.4 us and 2 431 B, and the
long-subject row shows it is fixed — the same anchored pattern on a
subject 18.8x longer allocates the same bytes to the digit, so it is not
a copy of the subject.

Repository suite: 8 219 tests across 13 projects, 0 failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
